  "step_3": "The decision reason involves comparing the mean difference between outcomes and a reference value (here, 0), which is equivalent to comparing expected values. The individual does not explicitly calculate expected values, but their reasoning focuses on the trade-off between a 1% higher probability of getting 0€ and a 1% higher chance of gaining 100€ more — which corresponds to comparing the net effect of the outcome differences (100€) and their probabilities (1% difference). Although the individual misstates the probabilities (claiming Lottery B has a 1% higher chance of the higher payout, when actually Lottery A has a higher probability of the higher amount), the structure of the reasoning — comparing the relative likelihoods of outcomes and their differences — aligns with the decision reason's logic. The individual evaluates differences in outcomes (100€) and differences in probabilities (1%) and weighs them in favor of the lottery with the more favorable combination. While the factual accuracy is flawed, the underlying cognitive process matches the decision reason’s rule: assessing the mean difference (or expected gain) based on outcome differences and probabilities. Therefore, it is reasonable to conclude that the individual used the decision reason, despite a misstatement of probabilities. The assessment is not certain due to the error, but the core reasoning pattern matches.",
  "final_assessment_score": 80
}

